Subject: [PATCH] fritchii2-bankflag: a bind of a native 64 KiB uniform bank marks the pages that guest CPU writes

fritchii2-bankflag: a bind of a native 64 KiB uniform bank marks the pages that guest CPU writes
were collected for, and that still wait for the next gather, as CPU-modified, so that bind uploads
the guest's current bytes. Switch EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_BANKFLAG=1, off by default.

Upstream: Frizti/Fritchii 9e8cb73ede (Frizti, 2026-11-07, EDEN_NX2_EXP9: Core::DevicePageFlags,
g_collected_write_pages and its Mark after Collect in HandleRasterizerWrite), with 1a64fe5002 and
29c7b12286 (EXP9=3 by default), released in fritchii-v4 and v5. Upstream EXP9 filters the per-bind
snapshot compare (d79572d28a / Lichii PR 345 a1b555012a, our 0245 EDEN_NX2_BANK_REUPLOAD=4, which
was worse at the pond in run329): only flagged pages are compared. This port keeps only the part
aimed at the mechanism, the collected-write flags. With no snapshots to compare, a flagged page of
a native bank is simply marked CPU-modified at the bind.
Not ported: the snapshot compare, g_buffer_write_pages (WriteMemory, inline and download writes
reach the tracker anyway; upstream needed them only to refresh snapshots), the hot-page and
frame-check rules, the node map (8b7bb3fab5), EXP9=2 verify and its 5 s stats. This neither
depends on nor changes EDEN_NX2_BANK_REUPLOAD in any mode, including the default 1.

Why: see fritchii2-lastaddr. A guest write that HandleRasterizerWrite only collects (its page was
GPU-modified, or the core's last_address is stuck on it) reaches the buffer cache at the next
gather. Until then the tracker holds the page clean, and a bind uploads nothing new. Eglantine
031e07436c (kraibse) fixed the resulting white material in Ocarina of Time by re-uploading every
bank on every bind; Fritchii 9e8cb73ede found these collected writes were what that check caught.
This catches only them.

Details:
- Mark: in GPUDirtyMemoryManager::Collect, so both HandleRasterizerWrite paths are covered (the
  HELD_VS_DOWNLOAD one too). Upstream marked in memory.cpp right after Collect; Collect is the same
  point and leaves memory.cpp alone, so this diff and fritchii2-lastaddr apply in either order.
- Take: in BindHostGraphicsUniformBuffer, for a native bank (native tiling, the whole bank not
  GPU-modified) of size 0x10000. It runs after the BANK_REUPLOAD block and before SynchronizeBuffer
  (the UBO snapshot path returns earlier and is untouched). Pages already CPU-modified are not
  marked again. A flag is set before its write's bytes land (HandleRasterizerWrite runs before the
  store), so a page taken at one bind is marked once more at its next bind (upstream's `recheck`;
  the list is capped at 256 pages). A bank that is GPU-modified at the bind keeps its flags for a
  later bind; the gather writes those pages inline, by design.
- The 2 MiB of flags (one bit per 4 KiB of the 36-bit device space) are allocated at first use,
  so only with the switch.
- Log: "EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_BANKFLAG on" once, then at each power of two "N bank pages held clean
  with collected guest writes marked at a bind, R marked again one bind later, C flagged pages
  were CPU-modified already". N is an upper bound, not a count of stale draws: a flag is only
  cleared by a bank bind, so N also counts writes that were already applied without one (a
  gather that wrote a GPU-modified page inline, or an upload through another binding of the
  page). There are no snapshots to tell those apart. If N stays 0 for a session, the mechanism
  does not reach the banks in our build: retire the switch. N > 0 alone proves nothing; the
  flash count decides.
  (Review 9 Nov: wording corrected, and upstream's static_assert that the flags cover the
  device address space restored.)

Cost: on the guest cores, one relaxed load per collected write (fetch_or only for a page not yet
flagged). On the back, 16 relaxed loads and page-table lookups per 64 KiB bank bind, plus a short
list scan while the list is not empty. Each flagged page that is clean costs a 4 KiB re-upload,
and one more a bind later. The mark also moves the write epoch of its 4 MiB (see
EDEN_NX2_UBO_BANK_DIRECT), so nearby buffers are walked again at their next sync. A page the game
rewrites between every two draws is flagged at every bind, so it is uploaded at every bind, as
any page the tracker sees written would be. Cheap when N grows slowly; if N grows by thousands a
second, watch the walk fps. Upstream only used the flags to choose which pages to memcmp, so a
stale flag cost it a compare, not an upload. Upstream's verify mode (1a64fe5002) also found the
flags missed about one changed bank page per 15 s in Ocarina of Time, so they do not catch every
bank change; the once-a-frame bank re-mark stays as the backstop.

Bright or dark: it fixes too-OLD bank words (see fritchii2-lastaddr), the white-material kind. It
pulls guest bytes in at the bind, earlier than the gather would. Without the frame wait that adds
the too-new risk BANK_REUPLOAD=4 showed at the pond. With the full frame wait (the play env) the
game does not run a frame ahead, so test it with the wait on.

T83: rebased onto the T83 stack (context only: fritchii-churn and fritchii2-lastaddr changed neighbouring lines).

